.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1216 AM PDT Wed Oct 14 2026
(Today and tonight)

Upper level troughing continues to progress eastward with ridging
building just to our west over the Pacific Ocean. The warming trend
becomes more noticeable on Wednesday as ridging starts to push
inland and becomes the dominate force across our region. High
temperatures will be more seasonable in the upper 70s to low 80s
across the interior and 60s along the coastline. Morning low
temperatures will remain slightly chillier in the 40s to  50s
Wednesday morning and Thursday morning. Temperatures continue to
warm on Thursday with highs reaching into the 80s to low 90s across
the interior (5-10 degrees above normal) and 60s to 70s along the
coast. Little to no cloud cover is expected overnight with upper
level ridging keeping the marine layer mixed out. This in turn is
creating much drier daytime and overnight conditions, particularly
across the higher elevations.

Our main concern for the upcoming week continues to be the potential
for multiple rounds of offshore winds in the overnight through late
morning hours with emphasis on the North Bay Interior Mountains. In
the short term, offshore winds are expected Tuesday night into
Wednesday but they are expected to be weaker than what is expected
Thursday into Friday. The SFO-WMC pressure gradient shows it peaking
around -3 to -4 mb overnight tonight into Wednesday. This is a
fairly weak gradient with offshore winds not anticipated to be too
strong tonight and gusts peak around 20 mph for the North Bay
Interior Mountains tonight. The story is a little different heading
into Wednesday. The SFO-WMC gradient initially starts out fairly
weak (-3 to -4 mb) but it looks to strengthen to -8 to -9 mb by late
Thursday morning. This indicates more moderate offshore winds are
likely across the North Bay Interior Mountains with gusts between 30
to 35 mph likely. Why the changeup? To explain, we go back to our
synoptic setup. As the trough exits to our east, a part of it splits
off from the jet stream and becomes a cut-off low that gradually
retrogrades back towards the Desert Southwest Wednesday night into
Thursday morning. As the cut-off low settles over Southern
California, it will force the upper level ridging over Northern
California to take on a more positive tilt (northeast to southwest
orientation) which is more conducive for offshore flow across the
region. The combination of strengthening offshore winds Thursday and
dry daytime/overnight conditions will result in elevated fire
weather concerns for the higher elevations with emphasis on the
North Bay Interior Mountains. Elevated fire weather concerns will
continue through Friday with further discussion in the Long Term.

