.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1216 AM PDT Wed Oct 14 2026
(Thursday through Tuesday)

Moderate offshore flow continues across the higher elevations
Thursday afternoon through Friday morning with the SFO-WMC
gradient peaking around -10 to -11 mb Thursday night into Friday
morning. The NAM suggests further strengthening to -12 to -13 mb
is possible which could support stronger gusts than outlined in
the short term (currently forecast peak gusts of 30 to 40 mph).
The driest conditions are expected overnight Thursday into Friday
with overnight RH recoveries only reaching 25-35% across the
highest elevations and daytime RH values dropping back into the
10s to 20s. Winds gradually diminish late morning into the
afternoon as the pressure gradient relaxes. Elevated fire weather
conditions continue for the higher elevations through Friday with
conditions improving as winds decrease. If you are participating
in any activity involving fire be very cautious and make sure
sparks/flames do not escape as fuels are incredibly dry and ready
to go.

High temperatures peak Friday in the 80s to low 90s with patches of
upper 90s across the higher elevations and southern Santa Clara
Valley. Temperatures cool slightly into the 80s by Saturday with
only a few hot spots reaching the low 90s as the ridge relaxes and
troughing becomes more dominant. The cut-off low will then merge
with the jet stream and result in widespread troughing across the
West Coast late weekend into early next week. As this trough pushes
inland it will bring us our first weak storm system of the season.
Initial guidance shows a weak cold front passing through California
early next week that will bring both a return of below normal
temperatures in the 60s to low 70s, a chance for light rain, and
gusty onshore winds. Fall mornings return as well with morning lows
dropping back into the 40s again late weekend into early next week.
Initial guidance suggests a more widespread potential for light rain
(up to a few tenths of an inch) early to mid next week. Onshore
winds ramp up along the coast and across mountain gaps/passes Sunday
into mid next week. The current forecast does not currently meet
Wind Advisory criteria but it is something to keep an eye on as this
system approaches given the stronger winds already associated with
it.

